.\" GPL-2.0-only
.\" mtr-rs: man page for the privileged probe helper.
.TH MTR-PACKET 8 "2026-09-04" "mtr-rs" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mtr-packet \- send network probes for mtr and report the replies
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mtr-packet
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mtr-packet
is the privileged half of
.BR mtr (8).
It is started by the unprivileged client, reads one command per line on
standard input and writes one reply per line on standard output. It opens
its raw sockets first, drops every privilege (setgid, setuid, and all
capability sets bar a granted
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN ,
see
.BR PRIVILEGES ),
and only then starts serving. It exits when standard input
is closed and no probe is outstanding.
.PP
The line protocol is that of mtr 0.96: a request is
.IR "token command [key value]..." ,
where
.I token
is an integer echoed back in the reply. The commands are
.B check-support
(with
.BR "feature version" ", " ip-4 ", " ip-6 ", " send-probe ", " icmp ", " udp ", " tcp ", " sctp " or " mark ;
the reply is
.BR "feature-support support ok" ,
.BR no ,
or the version string) and
.B send-probe
(with
.BR "ip-4 ADDR" " or " "ip-6 ADDR" ,
and optionally
.BR "protocol icmp|udp|tcp|sctp" ", " "port N" ", " "local-port N" ", "
.BR "local-ip ADDR" ", " "local-device NAME" ", " "ttl N" ", " "size N" ", "
.BR "bit-pattern N" ", " "tos N" ", " "timeout SECONDS" ", " "mark N" ).
Probe replies are
.BR reply ", " ttl-expired " or " no-route-host ,
each followed by
.BR "ip-4|ip-6 ADDR round-trip-time MICROSECONDS"
and, when the router attached RFC 4950 MPLS objects,
.BR "mpls label,tc,s,ttl[,...]" .
A probe that gets no answer within its timeout yields
.BR no-reply .
Errors are reported by name
.RB ( invalid-argument ", " probes-exhausted ", " permission-denied ", "
.BR address-in-use ", " address-not-available ", " network-down ", "
.BR host-down ", " no-route-network ", " no-route-host ", "
.BR unexpected-error " errno N)."
A malformed line is answered
.B 0 command-parse-error
and an overlong one
.BR "0 command-buffer-overflow" .
.SH PRIVILEGES
Raw sockets need
.B CAP_NET_RAW
(install with
.BR "setcap cap_net_raw+ep /usr/bin/mtr-packet" ).
Without it
.B mtr-packet
falls back to unprivileged ICMP and UDP datagram sockets with
.BR IP_RECVERR ;
echo replies and time-exceeded errors are still reported, but MPLS labels
are not available on that path and TCP/SCTP probes cannot see intermediate
hops.
.PP
The privilege drop keeps
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN ,
and only that, when it was actually granted, because
.B SO_MARK
is set per probe after the drop:
.BR mtr (8)'s
.B \-M
.RB ( \-\-mark )
therefore needs
.B cap_net_admin
on
.B mtr-packet
and root (or
.BR cap_net_admin )
for the client's own route lookup. Grant both with
.BR "setcap cap_net_raw,cap_net_admin+ep /usr/bin/mtr-packet" .
.B "check\-support feature mark"
answers
.B ok
only when the capability is really held.
.PP
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
is one of the broadest Linux capabilities \(em interface and routing
configuration, netfilter, promiscuous mode, netlink administration \(em so it
grants far more than
.BR SO_MARK ,
and because the drop happens before the first command is read, a helper that
started with it keeps it for the whole run, on every run and not only when
.B \-M
is used; the same holds under
.BR "sudo mtr" .
Grant it only if you use
.BR \-M .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mtr.is.run.under.sudo
When present, the
.BR mtr (8)
client ignores
.B $MTR_PACKET
and
.BR $MTR_RS_LOG ,
refuses
.BR \-F ", " \-\-config " and " \-\-init-config ,
and does not read the default user configuration file.  These are the
environment- or argument-controlled paths that the client can otherwise
execute, read, or create.  The marker therefore prevents a sudoers rule permitting
.B mtr
from being used for arbitrary path access; it does not drop the client's root
privileges or restrict which network targets and probe options the caller may
select.  The sudo policy should still sanitise the environment and grant only
the intended command line.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mtr (8)
.SH AUTHORS
mtr-rs is a Rust port of mtr by Matt Kimball, Roger Wolff and others; the
protocol and behaviour follow mtr 0.96.
